== Conclusion
<sec:simulator:conclusion>

This chapter lays the foundation of the acoustics paradigm leveraged in this thesis.
It introduced key theoretical notions in acoustics, signal processing, and simulation.
This framework is essential to the experimental scientific work conducted in the rest of this project.
After presenting the relevant background for understanding how sound propagates in reverberant environments, we gave an overview of the existing techniques used to model this paradigm.
The literature comprises diverse methods that address this problem with various tradeoffs.
In this regard, this chapter attempts to bridge the gap between the physical phenomenon of sound propagation and effective computational solutions for simulating it.
Next, a survey of the available software implementations was conducted, highlighting the efforts of a vibrant research community.

Finally, we presented this thesis's first contribution: a complex and feature-rich pipeline for simulating diverse acoustic scenarios.
We focused on developing a user-friendly toolbox, specifically targeting researchers.
It aims to be comprehensive and complete while allowing for a user-friendly experience.
This significant engineering effort resulted in the experimental setup for developing and testing learning algorithms with flexibility.
As highlighted in the introduction of this chapter, simulation has proven to be greatly useful in science, and especially in the fields of robotics and statistical learning.
